==================================================================
DAY 42 - PART B: LAB (CHECKPOINT)
Fix a randomly broken cluster in 30 minutes
Time: 60-90 minutes
==================================================================

STEP 1: Snapshot all VMs ("before-chaos")
-----------------------------------------
If you get completely stuck, you can restore.


STEP 2: Deploy a "canary" app that must work at the end
-------------------------------------------------------
k create deploy canary --image=YOURUSER/myapp:v1 --replicas=3
k expose deploy canary --type=NodePort --port=80 --target-port=5000
NP=$(k get svc canary -o jsonpath='{.spec.ports[0].nodePort}') ; echo $NP
curl worker1:$NP


STEP 3: The chaos script (written by you, run without reading)
--------------------------------------------------------------
Ideally a friend writes it. Otherwise create it, then don't look at
which faults it chose. Save as ~/chaos.sh on cp1. It uses ssh to the
workers, so set up key-based ssh from cp1 to worker1/worker2 first
(ssh-keygen ; ssh-copy-id worker1 ; ssh-copy-id worker2) and make sure
your user has sudo without a password prompt for the lab, or run the
worker faults by hand.

#!/bin/bash
# chaos.sh - applies 3 random faults and records them in /root/.chaos
faults=(
 "ssh worker1 sudo systemctl stop kubelet"
 "ssh worker2 sudo systemctl stop containerd"
 "kubectl scale deploy coredns -n kube-system --replicas=0"
 "kubectl taint node worker1 chaos=true:NoExecute"
 "kubectl patch svc canary -p '{\"spec\":{\"selector\":{\"app\":\"canaryX\"}}}'"
 "kubectl set image deploy/canary myapp=YOURUSER/myapp:nope"
 "kubectl cordon worker2"
 "sudo sed -i 's/- kube-scheduler$/- kube-schedulerX/' /etc/kubernetes/manifests/kube-scheduler.yaml"
 "ssh worker1 \"sudo sed -i 's/cgroupDriver: systemd/cgroupDriver: cgroupfs/' /var/lib/kubelet/config.yaml && sudo systemctl restart kubelet\""
 "kubectl delete endpointslice -l kubernetes.io/service-name=kubernetes"
)
for i in $(shuf -i 0-$((${#faults[@]}-1)) -n 3); do
  echo "${faults[$i]}" | sudo tee -a /root/.chaos >/dev/null
  eval "${faults[$i]}" >/dev/null 2>&1
done
echo "3 faults applied. Start your 30-minute timer."

(The image fault assumes the container is called "myapp"; check with
 k get deploy canary -o jsonpath='{.spec.template.spec.containers[0].name}'
 The endpointslice fault self-heals: a good lesson in reconciliation.)

bash ~/chaos.sh


STEP 4: Fix within 30 minutes
-----------------------------
Start a timer. Use only the method from Day 41. Write each finding in
Part C as you go.

Done when ALL are true:
  [ ] k get nodes: all Ready, none SchedulingDisabled
  [ ] k get pods -A: nothing failing
  [ ] k run t --rm -it --image=busybox:1.36 -- nslookup kubernetes.default  works
  [ ] curl worker1:$NP and curl worker2:$NP return your app
  [ ] k get deploy canary: 3/3


STEP 5: Check your answers
--------------------------
sudo cat /root/.chaos
Compare with what you found. Run the chaos script again on another day
for more practice (or restore the snapshot and retry).


